 ## Overview
-G-NeLF is a grid-based Neural Light Field (NeLF) method for novel view synthesis that predicts ray color directly without point-wise volume density modeling [S1, unofficial]. It represents a ray as a sequence of spatial-aware features extracted from a hash-based multi-resolution tri-plane, which are decoded by an LSTM network to predict the final color [S1, unofficial]. Unlike standard NeRF methods that model density and color per point, G-NeLF leverages the temporal order of ray propagation to capture view-dependent appearance with significantly fewer parameters [S1, unofficial]. It is distinguished from its siblings by its specific use of a tri-plane hash grid combined with a recurrent LSTM decoder, enabling high-quality rendering with extremely small model sizes (e.g., 0.95 MB) and training on limited views (≤100) without requiring pseudo-view synthesis [S1, unofficial].
+G-NeLF is a grid-based Neural Light Field (NeLF) method for novel view synthesis that predicts ray color directly without point-wise volume density modeling [S1, unofficial]. It represents a ray as a sequence of spatial-aware features extracted from a hash-based multi-resolution tri-plane, which are decoded by an LSTM network to predict color [S1, unofficial]. Unlike standard NeRF methods that model density and color per point, G-NeLF bypasses explicit physical modeling to achieve high performance with significantly fewer parameters (e.g., 0.95 MB) and less training data (≤100 views) [S1, unofficial]. It sets itself apart from sibling NeLF methods like R2L by using a spatial-aware feature sequence instead of direct coordinate concatenation, and from grid-based NeRFs like Instant-NGP by using a ray-wise representation that requires roughly one-tenth the parameters [S1, unofficial].
 
